<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Song of Hannah</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">not known; set in the narrative before Israel had a king</span> · Also known as שירת חנה, Shirat Hannah</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> of Hannah is a poem set within the prose story of the Books of Samuel. According to the surrounding narrative, the poem (1 Samuel 2:1–10) was a prayer that Hannah spoke to give thanks to God for the birth of her son, Samuel. Hannah praises <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yhwh">Yahweh</a>, reflects on the reversals he brings about and looks forward to his king.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The song moves from the particular to the general. It opens with Hannah's own gratitude for a local reversal and closes with God's defeat of his enemies, a cosmic reversal. Between them it declares that the bows of the mighty are broken while those who stumbled are girded with strength, that the full hire themselves out for bread while the hungry no longer hunger, and that the barren woman has borne seven. It is a hymn of praise for good fortune that takes up many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theme-system">themes</a> of Israel's national culture, and it counts fertility and childbirth as worthy of Israel's singers alongside its other motifs. It is similar to Psalm 113.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Through its theme of reversal the song serves as an introduction to the whole Book of Samuel. The commentators Keil and Delitzsch read Hannah's experience as a pledge of how God would lift up his whole nation, then oppressed by its foes, and Walter Brueggemann sees in it a major theme of the book, the power and willingness of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yhwh">Yahweh</a> to intrude, intervene and invert. The mention of a king in verse 10 has provoked discussion: A. F. Kirkpatrick argued that it does not imply a late date, since the desire for a king was probably already taking shape amid the anarchy of the time. The blessings on the king and the anointed parallel 2 Samuel 22.</p><p>Some contributors to classical rabbinic literature read the first half of the poem as a prophecy of Samuel's later role, of a great-grandson who would be a musician in the Jerusalem Temple, of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sennacherib">Sennacherib</a>'s destruction of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-israel">Kingdom of Israel</a>, of the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nebuchadnezzar-ii">Nebuchadnezzar</a> and of the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonian-captivity">Babylonian Captivity</a>. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a> the song is the model for reverent personal prayer and is read as the haftarah on the first day of Rosh Hashanah.</p><p>The poem shares several features with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnificat">Magnificat</a>, and a number of Christian scholars believe that the apostle Luke used it as the basis of that hymn; Charles Anang and others see Hannah as a type of Mary. In Roman Catholicism it is the Canticle of Anna, one of seven <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-testament">Old Testament</a> canticles in the breviary, sung at Lauds on Wednesdays. In the Revised Common Lectionary it is the response to the first lesson for proper 28 in year B, track 1.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Song of Hannah” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Magnificat echoes many passages of the Hebrew Bible, above all the <b>Song of Hannah</b> in 1 Samuel.</blockquote><a href="/songs/magnificat">Magnificat</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Song of Hannah</b> is a poem set within the prose story of the Books of Samuel.</blockquote><a href="/songs/song-of-hannah">Song of Hannah</a></li></ul></section>
